    <article class="article">
      <h1 class="section-title">What Sifan Hassan teaches us about resilience</h1>
      <p class="article-meta"><svg style="width:1em;height:1em;vertical-align:-0.15em;margin-right:0.3em" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><path d="M16 2v4M8 2v4M3 10h18"/></svg>19 June 2026 · <span class="accent"><svg style="width:1em;height:1em;vertical-align:-0.15em;margin-right:0.25em" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 2l2.9 6.26 6.88.56-5.2 4.52 1.56 6.7L12 17.27 5.86 20.54l1.56-6.7-5.2-4.52 6.88-.56z"/></svg>Champions</span> · 4 min read</p>

      <picture>
        <source type="image/avif" srcset="images/cat-champions.avif?v=1">
        <source type="image/webp" srcset="images/cat-champions.webp?v=1">
        <img src="images/cat-champions.jpg?v=1" alt="Running shoe laces: a symbol of resilience in running" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="740" height="494">
      </picture>
      <p>
        Tokyo, 2 August 2021. A 1500m heat at the Olympic Games. On the final lap, a runner in
        front of her trips and <strong>Sifan Hassan</strong> goes down with her. By the time she
        gets up, the pack is already well ahead.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then she starts running. Over the last 200 metres she passes her rivals one by one and
        finishes <strong>first</strong>. That same evening she runs the 5000m final and wins gold.
      </p>
      <!-- YOUR OWN LINE: If you watched the race live or the video, add one sentence here about
           how it felt. E.g. "I've watched the video of that fall over and over..." -->
      <p>
        By the end of those Games she had three medals: gold in the 5000m and 10,000m, bronze in
        the 1500m. Three different distances, in one week.
      </p>

      <h3>From Ethiopia to the Netherlands</h3>
      <p>
        Sifan was born in Ethiopia in 1993 and arrived in the Netherlands at 15, as a refugee.
        That's where she started running, and within a few years she was at the top of the world
        in the 1500m. She could have stayed there, at the distance she knew well. She chose to
        keep moving up: 5000m, 10,000m and, in the end, the marathon.
      </p>

      <h3>London, 2023: her first marathon</h3>
      <p>
        She ran her first marathon while fasting for Ramadan. Around the middle of the race she
        stopped twice to stretch her hip while the others pulled away. Many thought her race was
        over. She caught them one by one and won in the sprint.
      </p>
      <p>
        She has admitted many times that she feels fear and doubt before a big race. She still
        shows up at the start line, every time.
      </p>

      <h3>Paris, 2024</h3>
      <p>
        At the Paris Olympics she did something that seems almost impossible: she ran the 5000m,
        the 10,000m and the marathon at the same Games. She took bronze in the first two. On the
        final day she won <strong>gold in the marathon</strong>, in an Olympic record, in a duel
        decided in the last few metres.
      </p>

      <h3>What I take from her story</h3>
      <p>
        We all fall at some point. In a bad workout, an injury, a stretch when nothing goes the
        way we wanted. Sifan shows us what you do right after: you get up and keep going, even if
        the pack is far ahead.
      </p>
      <!-- YOUR OWN LINE: A "fall" of your own in running (injury, bad race, a break) and how you
           found your rhythm again. Two or three sentences are enough. -->
      <p>
        Do you remember a time you "fell" in your running? What made you get back up?
      </p>

